Your job is to produce a single `<screen_collarch>` block containing
nine sections in a fixed order: a role-level technical specification,
the collection's public surface (its routes and layout-slot contract),
its private surface, its failure surface, what it mints as policy
considerations, the journeys it consumes, its subcomponent
decomposition, the dependency edges between subcomponents, and
attached references. Which UI collections it renders and which backend
components it calls are `frontend_sysarch`'s own decisions (shown to
you as input above), not this document's to redeclare.

# Output format

Emit exactly one `<screen_collarch>` block with these children in this
order: `<technical-specification>` → `<public-surface>` →
`<private-surface>` → `<failure-surface>` → `<policies>` →
`<journeys>` (optional) →
`<subcomponents>` → `<sub-dependencies>` → `<references>` (optional).
Example (abbreviated):

    <screen_collarch>
      <technical-specification>
    A LiveView per hosted screen, mounted under one live_session
    sharing the checkout layout. Each mount loads the in-progress
    subscription draft (if any) from Billing and assigns it whole;
    state transitions dispatch through Billing's boundary exports and
    re-render from the returned draft rather than mutating local
    assigns speculatively.
      </technical-specification>
      <public-surface>
    ```
    live "/plans", PlanSelectLive, :show
    live "/plans/:draft_id/payment", PaymentDetailsLive, :show
    live "/plans/:draft_id/confirm", SubscribeConfirmLive, :show
    ```
    Layout slot: `:progress_indicator`, filled by every LiveView in
    this collection with the current checkout step.
      </public-surface>
      <private-surface>
    ```
    assign_draft(socket, draft) — shared helper installing the draft
    and its derived plan total into assigns.
    ```
      </private-surface>
      <failure-surface>
    A draft that expires between mount and payment submission surfaces
    as a typed `:draft_expired` error from Billing's confirm call,
    handled by redirecting to plan-select with a flash; this is not
    silent — the redirect and flash are asserted in tests.
      </failure-surface>
      <policies>
    No collection-local policy considerations.
      </policies>
      <journeys>
        <journey ref="journey_signup01"/>
      </journeys>
      <subcomponents>
        <subcomponent alias="plan_select_live">
          <name>PlanSelectLive</name>
          <purpose>Loads the plan roster and starts a draft on
          selection.</purpose>
          <owned-invariants>
            <invariant>selecting a plan always produces exactly one
            draft subscription, never zero or two</invariant>
          </owned-invariants>
          <primary-operations>
            <operation>load the plan roster on mount</operation>
            <operation>start a draft on plan selection</operation>
          </primary-operations>
          <responsibilities>Hosts the plan-select screen; the entry
          point for this collection's checkout flow.</responsibilities>
          <implementation grade="real"/>
        </subcomponent>
      </subcomponents>
      <sub-dependencies>
      </sub-dependencies>
    </screen_collarch>

# Rules

## Structure

* Emit **exactly one** `<screen_collarch>` root block. Nothing before,
  nothing after.
* The children **must appear in this order**: `<technical-specification>`
  → `<public-surface>` → `<private-surface>` → `<failure-surface>` →
  `<policies>` → `<journeys>`
  (omit entirely when empty) → `<subcomponents>` → `<sub-dependencies>`
  → `<references>` (omit entirely when nothing is attached).

## Fragment sections

* `<technical-specification>` names the mounting/routing shape (one
  LiveView per screen? a shared live_session? which layout?) and the
  data-loading pattern (what's loaded on mount vs. on an action).
* `<public-surface>` is this collection's **routes** (one per hosted
  screen, matching the screen's own slug) and its **layout-slot
  contract** — what a UI collection this screen renders needs handed
  down as assigns, and what shared layout slots this collection fills.
  This is the only thing a downstream reader (a later regeneration, a
  reviewer) sees of this collection's routing — be as concrete about
  the route pattern as the public-surface rules elsewhere in this
  chain ask of a function signature.
* `<private-surface>`, `<failure-surface>`, `<policies>` follow the
  same fragment discipline as `ui_collarch`'s own — all non-empty
  prose, code-shaped content in fenced blocks.
* **Every affordance named on a hosted screen must be grounded
  somewhere in this document** — as a route, a dispatched call in
  `<technical-specification>`, or a subcomponent operation. An
  affordance with no path to a call or a route is a coverage gap the
  reviewer will flag.

## Rendering and calling backend components

* **Which UI collections this collection renders and which backend
  components it calls are `frontend_sysarch`'s own decisions**, not
  this document's — that pass mints every collection and sees the
  whole backend surface at once, the one moment those cross-family
  relationships can be named correctly. You read both back as input
  (the UI-collections and backend-components lists above) and ground
  `<technical-specification>`/`<public-surface>` in them; you don't
  redeclare either structurally.
* **This is still the only tier whose design may call a backend
  component** — never delegate a call down into a UI collection's
  props; the UI collection receives the *result* of the call as an
  assign.

## Journeys

* `<journeys>` is optional — `<journey ref="..."/>` for each journey,
  from the full pool above, whose live state this collection actually
  reads or writes (a draft's progress, a cart). Omit or leave empty
  when this collection consumes no journey state; most screen
  collections outside a multi-step flow will.

## Subcomponents

* Same shape as `ui_collarch`'s own subcomponents — `alias`, `<name>`,
  `<purpose>`, `<owned-invariants>` (2-4), `<primary-operations>`
  (3-6), `<responsibilities>` (free prose), `<implementation
  grade="real"|"stubbed"/>`. No `<owns>` block: a screen collection
  fulfills no `resp` either — its structural link to the product tier
  is the `fulfills` edge naming which screens it hosts, not a resp
  claim.
* One subcomponent per hosted LiveView is the common shape, but not a
  rule — a collection whose screens share enough mounting/data logic
  that splitting by screen would just duplicate a helper may
  legitimately use fewer subcomponents than screens, with the shared
  logic living in one of them and the others depending on it.

## Sub-dependencies

* `<sub-dependencies>` lists edges between this collection's own
  subcomponents, `<dep from="alias" to="alias"/>`. Must be acyclic.

## References

* `<references>` is optional — omit entirely when nothing is attached.

## Meta-rules

* Do not include commentary about what you are doing or how you
  arrived at the design. Output only the `<screen_collarch>` block.
* Unescaped `&` and `<` inside fragment-section text are tolerated by
  the parser.
